`
The interpreter ignores:
blank lines
newlines
indents or spaces before lines
text between grave accents (comments)

Semicolons (;) should not be necessary on empty lines or lines which
Are entirely taken up by comments because the interpreter ignores them.

Spaces are an important part of how the interpreter functions. They separate different parts of the command.
The first "chunk" is the name of a command which will trigger either an internal command (C code in the interpreter)
or a Bronze command / function. The following are arguments, which will depend on the command.

Datatypes are indicated by the keyword before the colon (:) in-between the keyword and the value respectively.
"str" tells the interpreter that the following is a string.
"copy" tells the interpreter that the following is the name of a variable, and it's value should be "copied".
The same applies for other common datatypes like "float" and higher-level datatypes like "str".
It is important that there are no spaces between the colon and the value or type keyword.
`

`
Here, the string "Hello" is assigned to the new variable "testvar" using the built-in "make" function.
The variable's datatype will be restricted to the datatype of the initial value, in this case a string.
`
make testvar str:Hello;

`The value can be changed with the built in "mod" function, which is short for "modify".
The datatype must be the same as the previous.

When defining strings, parentheses should be used if the string contains spaces. This
tells the interpreter not to interpret the spaces as ending the value argument, but to
include them. This applies anywhere where an argument value contains spaces.
`
mod testvar str:(Hello again);

`Here, the variable's value is copied into the built-in "log" command, which will print it out or "log" it.`
log copy:testvar;

`A unique feature of this programming language will be the ability to dynamically change
the line of code which is being run using mark and return commands`

`mark line with name "loop"`
mark str:loop;
    log str:(a very weird loop);
if ref:your_condition_here;
    `Return to marked line named "loop"`
    return str:loop;
endif;

`"ref" (or "reference") may be used instead of "copy" to check the value of the boolean every loop.`

make condition bool:true;
if copy:condition;
    `The builtin "if" command will toggle a variable in the interpreter's C code which tells the interpreter to only run
    The following lines if the supplied condition is true. This will apply to all lines below this statement until
    the builtin "endif" command, which does the opposite. `

    log str:(This will log because the condition is true);

    `Indents can be added purely for looks, but they are not strictly necessary as the interpreter ignores them.`
endif;